## Steps and evidence

### STEP 0 -- a WORKING mirror must exist before removal can mean anything
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
host# ./cap.sh rm-before      (cap-rm-before.txt, ping-rm-before.txt)
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 6 ; echo REPLIES = 0 ; ping 6/6, 0% packet loss
  PASS. The mirror is live and demonstrably copying frames, so a later absence of frames is
  attributable to the removal and not to a mirror that never worked.

### STEP 1 -- the BARE `no mirror interface` is rejected, and changes nothing
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
      <-- advisory on ENTERING the interface, not a rejection
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface
  % Incomplete command.                   <-- the bare form IS rejected by the parser
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48         <-- UNCHANGED: the rejection removed nothing
  => STEP 1 PASS: `no mirror interface` without a source argument is refused with
     `% Incomplete command.` and the existing mirror survives the attempt intact. The drafted
     expectation is confirmed, and the rejection is recorded as evidence rather than worked
     around. (The syntax requires an argument: triage measured `no mirror interface ?` offering
     `IFRANGE` or `none`.)

### STEP 2 -- the correct form removes it from the configuration
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
  % port1.0.10 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
      <-- advisory; tools/cfg.py logged "(advisory, not an error -- continuing)" and carried on
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
                                          <-- empty
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
  interface port1.0.10
   switchport
   switchport mode access                 <-- the mirror line is gone; plain access port again
  => STEP 2 PASS: the removal is accepted and leaves no residue in the running-config.

### STEP 3 -- frame-level proof that mirroring actually STOPPED
host# ./cap.sh rm-after       (cap-rm-after.txt, ping-rm-after.txt)
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0 ; ping 6/6, 0% packet loss
  => STEP 3 PASS: the identical flow that produced 6 mirrored frames in STEP 0 now produces
     none, while still passing end to end. Mirroring stopped in the dataplane, not merely in
     the configuration -- which matters on this platform, because M2 and M3 show that the
     configuration display and the dataplane can disagree.

### STEP 4 -- the destination port RETURNED TO NORMAL SWITCHING
A mirror destination leaves every VLAN and stops participating in switching, so proving it
came back is part of proving the removal. `show interface status` cannot show this: it reports
a mirror destination identically with and without a mirror. A capture can.
host# tcpdump -i eth3 -n -c 5
  ... 09:36:11.954256 LLDP, length 50
  5 packets captured
  5 packets received by filter
  => STEP 4 PASS: with the mirror removed, eth3 again receives ordinary VLAN-1 traffic --
     LLDP and broadcast -- which it had not seen at all while port1.0.10 was a mirror
     destination. The port is back in normal switching. This is positive proof of the kind
     `show interface status` is incapable of giving.

VERDICT: PASS -- the bare `no mirror interface` is correctly rejected with `% Incomplete
command.` and leaves the existing mirror intact; `no mirror interface port1.0.48` removes it
from the running-config, stops the frame copying in the dataplane, and returns port1.0.10 to
normal switching. All four legs proven against a mirror that was demonstrably working first.
